# Copy to tools/credentials.env and fill in. That file is gitignored and must
# stay that way: it names WHERE your secrets live, and a vault path is itself
# something you do not publish.
#
# These are REFERENCES, never values. Every scheme gatewayd accepts works here:
#
#   env:BYBIT_API_KEY                    read from the environment
#   file:/run/secrets/bybit_key          read from a file
#   exec:op read op://vault/item/field   run a command and take its output
#   op://vault/item/field                shorthand for the same
#
# A venue with no entry is skipped by tools/private_smoke.sh rather than run
# without credentials — a smoke that quietly tests the public path is a smoke
# that reports success for a venue it never authenticated against.
#
# ON MACOS, IF EVERY RUN ASKS FOR PERMISSION TO CONTROL ANOTHER APPLICATION:
# the vault lives in a 1Password account the CLI is not signed into, so `op`
# reaches it by driving the desktop app over Apple Events, and the OS asks your
# terminal's parent each time. Sign the CLI into that account directly and the
# prompts stop:
#
#   op account add --address <your-account>.1password.com --email <you>
#   eval $(op signin)
#
# `op account list` shows which accounts the desktop app exposes; `op` on its
# own only knows the ones in ~/.config/op/config. Both tools here resolve every
# reference in ONE `op run` invocation, so even ungranted it is one prompt per
# start rather than one per credential.

# BYBIT_API_KEY=op://<vault>/<item>/<field>
# BYBIT_API_SECRET=op://<vault>/<item>/<field>

# BINANCE_API_KEY=
# BINANCE_API_SECRET=

# GATE_API_KEY=
# GATE_API_SECRET=

# XT_API_KEY=
# XT_API_SECRET=

# BINGX_API_KEY=
# BINGX_API_SECRET=

# These three sign with a passphrase as well; gatewayd refuses to start with
# only two of the three rather than rejecting each order in turn.
# OKX_API_KEY=
# OKX_API_SECRET=
# OKX_PASSPHRASE=

# KUCOIN_API_KEY=
# KUCOIN_API_SECRET=
# KUCOIN_PASSPHRASE=

# BITGET_API_KEY=
# BITGET_API_SECRET=
# BITGET_PASSPHRASE=
